  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1156 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nA low pressure system moving east through North Dakota today is \ntrailing a cold front into SD. Ahead of the front, south to \nsouthwest winds are gusting 30 to 40 mph. Winds will shift to the \nwest and then northwest behind the front. On the tail of the front, \nin the upper trough, some virga/sprinkles have developed across \ncentral SD. The clouds and the strong mixing winds are fighting it \nout for control of today's highs along the Missouri River, but those \nmodified highs are more likely down near Pierre given the faster \nshift to westerly and northwest winds with the abundant cloud cover. \nOtherwise, this system looks mostly dry, as well. Left headlines as-\nis with no major change in forecast reasoning. \n\nAs the system pulls off to the northeast on Thursday, winds will \ndiminish. Another Rocky Mountain low develops Thursday night with \nsoutherly winds picking up ahead of it. This low moves into South \nDakota and Nebraska on Friday. Gusty winds, mainly across the \nsouthern and eastern forecast area, appear to be the primary concern \nwith this upcoming low, too, as the majority of any associated \nprecip remains across ND and northern MN. Additionally, significant \ncaa is expected behind the Friday system. H85 temps fall around 10C \nthrough Saturday putting Sat highs in the 40s and Sunday's highs in \nthe 30s. \n\nBroad upper troughing and sfc high pressure then linger into next \nweek with the colder but more seasonable temperatures locked in \nthrough mid week.",
